# Batch 6 (2026-10-18): Africa, Asia, Oceania and the Americas weighted; ancient astronomy, writing, tombs, relics, collapses, sunken cities, mummies
H,M='history','myth'
def X(id,title,lane,status,pr,start,ev,main,alts,src,img=None,end=None,changed=None,dateNote=None):
  d=dict(id=id,title=title,lane=lane,status=status,priority=pr,start=start,end=end,ev=ev,main=main,alts=alts,src=src,img=img)
  if changed:d['changed']=changed
  if dateNote:d['dateNote']=dateNote
  return d
E=[
# --- part 1: Africa
X('lebombo-bone','Lebombo bone: a 43,000-year-old tally?',H,'confirmed',2,-43000,['archaeological','radiocarbon'],
 'A baboon fibula about 8 cm long with incised notches, found in Border Cave in the Lebombo Mountains between South Africa and Eswatini. Twenty-four radiocarbon dates put it at about 42,000–43,000 years old, far older than the Ishango bone it is often confused with. A 2012 reanalysis of Border Cave organic artefacts (d\'Errico et al.) concluded that its Early Later Stone Age inhabitants used notched bones "for notational purposes", alongside tools closely resembling those of later San hunter-gatherers.',
 [('Tally or calendar (interpretation)','The bone has been conjectured to be a tally stick; popular accounts sometimes call it a lunar calendar or the oldest mathematical object, which goes beyond what the notches alone can show.'),
  ('Ritual marks','Its discoverer, Peter Beaumont, saw the changes in notch section (made with different cutting edges) as evidence that the marks were made during participation in rituals, like other notched objects worldwide. Even older notched bones (c. 80,000 years) exist, but it is unclear whether such notches are decorative or functional.')],
 ['doi:10.1073/pnas.1204213109','wiki:Lebombo_bone'],'Border_Cave',
 dateNote='About 42,000–43,000 years old (24 radiocarbon dates)'),
X('green-sahara','The Green Sahara (African Humid Period)',H,'confirmed',2,-12600,['geological','palaeoclimate','archaeological'],
 'From about 14,600–14,500 years ago until roughly 6,000–5,000 years ago much of the Sahara was covered by grasses, trees and lakes, caused by changes in Earth\'s axial tilt, a strengthened African monsoon, vegetation and dust feedbacks and greenhouse gases. Lake Chad expanded, Lake Victoria refilled and the "Wild Nile" produced its largest recorded floods. People settled the desert, herded cattle, goats and sheep, and left rock art of swimmers and savanna animals in places that are now hyper-arid. Dry spells interrupted it during the Younger Dryas and the 8.2-kiloyear event; when it ended, people moved toward the Nile Valley and Mesopotamia, where early complex societies arose.',
 [('Abrupt end','deMenocal et al. (2000), using marine sediment off West Africa, argued that the humid period began and ended abruptly, within decades to centuries.'),
  ('Gradual end','Kröpelin et al. (2008), using a lake record from northern Chad, argued for a gradual drying over thousands of years; the end may have come in several steps, including around the 4.2-kiloyear event.'),
  ('Link to Egypt and myth (interpretation)','Researchers connect the drying with the rise of Ancient Egyptian civilisation; some also suggest that former lakes such as Nabta Playa may relate to later Egyptian religion. Alternative writers sometimes cite the wetter Sahara in support of an older Sphinx (see Sphinx water-erosion hypothesis), which mainstream Egyptology does not accept.')],
 ['doi:10.1016/S0277-3791(99)00081-5','doi:10.1126/science.1154913','wiki:African_humid_period'],'Cave_of_Swimmers',end=-3000,
 dateNote='c. 14,600 to c. 5,000 years ago, with interruptions',
 changed='1850: Heinrich Barth suggests past wetter climates from Saharan petroglyphs. 1920s–30s: rock art of swimmers and savanna animals strengthens the idea. Late 20th century: lake cores and marine sediments confirm and date the humid period.'),
X('tassili-round-heads','Tassili n\'Ajjer and the "Round Head" paintings',H,'confirmed',2,-7550,['archaeological','rock art'],
 'The Tassili n\'Ajjer plateau in the Algerian Sahara holds one of the world\'s great concentrations of rock art (UNESCO World Heritage Site). It is usually divided into Archaic (10,000–7500 BCE), Round Head (7550–5050 BCE), Bovidian/Pastoral, Horse and Camel periods. The Round Head paintings, by hunter-gatherers living in a green Sahara, include some of Africa\'s oldest and largest rock paintings (one figure over five metres tall) and show floating humanoid figures with round, featureless heads. Henri Lhote\'s 1950s expeditions made the art famous, but his team was later accused of faking images and of damaging paintings while brightening them for tracing and photography, reducing the original colours beyond repair.',
 [('Mushroom-ritual hypothesis (speculative)','Giorgio Samorini (1989) proposed that fungoid figures, such as masked dancers holding mushroom-like objects and the "mushroom shaman" of Matalem-Amazar, show ritual use of psychedelic mushrooms; Terence McKenna built on this in Food of the Gods (1992). Other researchers do not accept that the images depict mushrooms.'),
  ('Ritual sanctuaries','Lhote regarded the painted shelters as secret sanctuaries; the concentration of Round Head motifs at particular sites is taken by researchers to suggest centres of ritual and ceremony.')],
 ["url:https://whc.unesco.org/en/list/179|Tassili n'Ajjer (UNESCO)|institution","wiki:Tassili_n'Ajjer"],"Tassili_n'Ajjer",end=-5050,
 dateNote='Round Head period c. 7550–5050 BCE (style-based dating)'),
X('lydenburg-heads','Lydenburg Heads',H,'confirmed',2,500,['archaeological','radiocarbon'],
 'Seven hollow terracotta heads from Lydenburg, Mpumalanga, South Africa, among the oldest known Iron Age artworks south of the equator. Charcoal found with them dates to about 1,410 years ago (about 500 CE). Six have human features and one an animal-like snout; the two largest, big enough to be worn over a child\'s head, carry small figurines (perceived as lions). They may once have been covered in white slip and glittering specularite. A boy, Karl-Ludwig von Bezing, first found fragments while playing on his father\'s farm and later collected more in 1962 and 1966; they were reconstructed at the University of Cape Town.',
 [('Use','They may have been worn as masks or headdresses (small perforations suggest attachment to a framework) or were part of a significant structure; their exact purpose is unknown.')],
 ['wiki:Lydenburg_Heads'],'Lydenburg_Heads'),
X('igbo-ukwu','Igbo-Ukwu bronzes',H,'confirmed',2,850,['archaeological','radiocarbon'],
 'Bronze and copper objects of great intricacy from three sites at Igbo-Ukwu, Anambra State, Nigeria: Igbo Isaiah (a shrine, found in 1938 by villager Isaiah Anozie while digging beside his home), Igbo Richard (a burial chamber with a body in apparent regalia) and Igbo Jonah (a cache), excavated by Thurstan Shaw from 1959. Radiocarbon dates of about the 9th century CE made them the earliest known bronze casting in the region, centuries before other known bronzes there. Igbo-Ukwu was associated with the Kingdom of Nri, and more than 165,000 glass and carnelian beads point to long-distance trade reaching Egypt and North Africa.',
 [('Outside origin (early speculation)','The technical skill and lack of known prototypes led to early academic speculation that the bronzes must post-date European contact, and "phantom voyagers" were postulated as their makers.'),
  ('Local origin (current view)','Isotope analysis shows the metals are of local origin, and radiocarbon dating confirmed a 9th-century date; a later AMS date on a textile from Igbo Isaiah (1027–1180 CE) suggests some material may be younger.')],
 ['wiki:Igbo-Ukwu','wiki:Archaeology_of_Igbo-Ukwu'],'Igbo-Ukwu',
 dateNote='Radiocarbon c. 850 CE (9th century)'),
X('sungbo-eredo','Sungbo\'s Eredo and the Queen of Sheba',H,'disputed',2,1000,['archaeological','oral tradition','radiocarbon'],
 'A system of ditches and banks more than 160 km long ringing the old Ijebu kingdom near Ijebu Ode, Ogun State, Nigeria; in places the height from ditch bottom to bank top reaches 20 m. Archaeologist Patrick Darling (1999) called it the largest single monument in Africa. Its dating is disputed: some carbon dates go back to 2000 BCE (which need not date the ditch), Darling proposed the 10th–11th centuries CE, and more recent excavations point to the late 14th–early 15th century. It probably served defence and the unification of diverse communities, like similar earthworks around Ife, Ilesa and Benin. LiDAR mapping began in 2024.',
 [('Bilikisu Sungbo','Ijebu tradition links the Eredo to a wealthy, childless widow, Oloye Bilikisu Sungbo, who built it as her memorial; her grave is believed to be at Oke-Eiri, where Christians and Muslims make an annual pilgrimage.'),
  ('Queen of Sheba','Some identify Bilikisu with the Queen of Sheba (Bilqis in Islamic tradition). Darling said the idea "cannot be discounted" because local people believe it, but that "the most cogent argument against it at the moment is the dating".')],
 ["wiki:Sungbo's_Eredo"],None,
 dateNote='Disputed: 10th–11th century CE (Darling) or late 14th–15th century CE'),
X('adams-calendar','"Adam\'s Calendar" and the Bokoni stone ruins',H,'disputed',2,1500,['archaeological','speculative'],
 'Thousands of stone-walled homesteads, cattle paths and agricultural terraces stretch for about 150 km along the Mpumalanga escarpment, South Africa. Archaeologists attribute them to Bokoni, an agro-pastoral society of about 1500–1820 CE that traded farm produce widely and was disrupted during the Mfecane; radiocarbon dates also showed the Lydenburg Bokoni sites predate the Pedi, overturning an older attribution. One stone circle complex, the Blaauboschkraal ruins near Emgwenya (a provincial heritage site), is popularly called "Adam\'s Calendar". Many sites are on private land and threatened by stone removal and vandalism.',
 [('Ancient or alien builders (speculative)','Michael Tellinger, influenced by Zecharia Sitchin\'s ancient-astronaut ideas (Sitchin\'s Anunnaki), is the most prominent proponent of alien and/or far more ancient origins for the site; with Johan Heine he has claimed the sites align with Great Zimbabwe and the Pyramids of Egypt and act as portals to other worlds. These claims are classed as pseudoarchaeology by researchers.'),
  ('Other non-local theories','Earlier, Hromník claimed Indian and "Indo-San" builders (dubbed "the exotic theory" by local researchers), and some proposed the sites were observatories, often linked to non-African construction; these ideas have mostly been rejected.')],
 ["wiki:Bokoni","wiki:Adam's_Calendar"],"Adam's_Calendar",end=1820),
X('thonis-heracleion','Thonis-Heracleion: the sunken port of Egypt',H,'confirmed',2,-600,['archaeological','textual'],
 'An Egyptian port near the Canopic mouth of the Nile, now under about 6 m of water in Abu Qir Bay, about 7 km offshore. It was Egypt\'s main port for international trade in the Late Period (flourishing 6th–4th centuries BCE), with a great temple of Amun-Gereb. It declined as Alexandria rose; around the 2nd century BCE soil liquefaction, floods and earthquakes collapsed the central island, and the last remains sank by the end of the 8th century CE. Located by Franck Goddio\'s IEASM team with Egypt\'s antiquities ministry after a five-year search, finds include a Nectanebo I decree stele (found in 2000) proving that "Thonis" and "Heracleion" were one city, statues of Serapis and Arsinoe II, a baris river boat matching Herodotus\'s description, and a 25 m Ptolemaic galley (announced 2021).',
 [('Legend of Paris and Helen','Herodotus tells that Paris and Helen of Troy, fleeing Menelaus, landed at the Canopic mouth, where the watchman Thonis seized them; in another version Menelaus and Helen stayed there. Whether such stories preserve any memory is unknown, but the city they named was real.'),
  ('Before 2000','Most historians had thought Thonis and Heracleion were two separate cities on what is now the Egyptian mainland.')],
 ['wiki:Thonis-Heracleion'],'Thonis-Heracleion',end=800,
 dateNote='Flourished 6th–4th centuries BCE; fully submerged by the late 8th century CE',
 changed='Known only from Greek authors until located underwater around 2000; excavations continue (finds announced 2019, 2021, 2023).'),
X('dendera-light','The "Dendera light"',H,'disputed',2,-54,['archaeological','textual','speculative'],
 'Reliefs in the crypts of the Hathor temple at Dendera, Egypt (temple begun 54 BCE under Ptolemy XII; some crypt reliefs date to his reign). They show the god Harsomtus as a snake emerging from a lotus flower, sometimes inside an oval container (hn), supported by a djed pillar or a kneeling figure. The accompanying hieroglyphic captions identify them as cult statues of Harsomtus, give their sizes (three palms to one cubit) and materials (gold, metal, precious stones), and relate to a creation myth; the crypts were treated as the underworld (Duat), where the gods\' statues were kept.',
 [('Ancient electric lamp (speculative)','A fringe theory holds that the reliefs depict ancient Egyptian electric technology, citing visual similarity to light bulbs, Crookes or Geissler tubes, and the lack of soot in tombs.'),
  ('Mainstream reply','Egyptologists point to the hieroglyphic captions. Bolko Stern showed that copper-topped poles mentioned in temple texts were magical, not electrical, and Kenneth Feder notes that no glass, sockets or filaments have ever been found; soot-free lighting could be explained by mirrors or by adding salt to torches.')],
 ['wiki:Dendera_light','wiki:Dendera_Temple_complex'],'Dendera_light'),
X('rain-queen','Modjadji, the Rain Queen',H,'confirmed',2,1600,['oral tradition','ethnographic'],
 'The hereditary queen of the Balobedu in Limpopo, South Africa, believed to be able to control clouds and rainfall: bringing rain to allies and drought to enemies. Origin stories trace the line to a chief\'s daughter, Dzugundini, who fled 16th-century Monomotapa (Zimbabwe) to the Molototsi Valley. By custom the queen avoids public functions, speaks through councillors, presides over an annual November rainmaking ceremony, and does not marry but receives "wives" from subject villages as a diplomatic tie. Eileen and Jack Krige\'s The Realm of a Rain-Queen (1943) remains a standard study. Her gardens hold the Modjadji cycad, named after her. Masalanabo Modjadji VII took the throne in 2023 after a succession dispute.',
 [('Belief and politics','Neighbouring communities, and even apartheid-era presidents, historically treated the queen with deference; anthropologists study the rain-making role as a basis of authority and diplomacy.'),
  ('Literary echo','The second Rain Queen, Masalanabo Modjadji, is said to have inspired H. Rider Haggard\'s novel She, and the title "She-who-must-be-obeyed".')],
 ['wiki:Rain_Queen'],'Encephalartos_transvenosus',
 dateNote='Origin traditions set in the 16th century; the queenship continues today'),
# --- part 2: East Africa, China, India, Korea, Japan, Philippines
X('tanganyika-laughter','Tanganyika laughter epidemic',H,'confirmed',2,1962,['documentary','medical reports'],
 'On 30 January 1962 three girls at a mission-run girls\' boarding school in Kashasha, on the western shore of Lake Victoria in Tanganyika (now Tanzania), began laughing uncontrollably. It spread to 95 of the 159 pupils; symptoms (laughter and crying, restlessness, pain, fainting, breathing problems, rashes) lasted hours to 16 days. The school closed on 18 March, and a second wave hit when it reopened in May. The episode spread to the village of Nshamba (217 people in April–May), to Ramashenye girls\' middle school and to other places within about 100 miles of Bukoba. About 1,000 people were affected and 14 schools shut before it faded after some 18 months. It is generally classed as mass psychogenic illness.',
 [('Stress of independence','Linguist Christian Hempelmann theorised the outbreak was stress-induced: Tanganyika had just become independent and pupils reported pressure from raised expectations; he notes such illness tends to affect people with little power.'),
  ('Cultural dissonance','Sociologist Robert Bartholomew and psychiatrist Simon Wessely proposed a culture-specific epidemic hysteria arising from conflict between conservative home values and the new ideas of missionary schools.')],
 ['wiki:Tanganyika_laughter_epidemic'],None,end=1963),
X('liangzhu','Liangzhu: a Neolithic state drowned by floods?',H,'confirmed',2,-3300,['archaeological','palaeoclimate'],
 'The Liangzhu culture (c. 3300–2300 BCE) of the Yangtze River Delta was the last great Neolithic jade culture of China and is often considered one of East Asia\'s earliest state societies. Its capital near Hangzhou had 290 hectares within clay walls with six gates, a 30-hectare palace complex (Mojiaoshan), canals, docks, workshops, and dams about 8 km to the north interpreted as an ancient flood-control system. Jade, silk, ivory and lacquer appear only in elite graves. UNESCO inscribed the Archaeological Ruins of Liangzhu City in 2019. After about 2300 BCE the culture vanishes from the Taihu Lake area.',
 [('Floods and rain','Cultural layers interrupted by muddy, marshy and sandy layers suggest repeated flooding; a 2021 cave-record study (Zhang et al.) found decades of extreme rainfall around 4,345–4,324 years ago, perhaps linked to more frequent El Niño conditions, and concluded that flooding may have forced the abandonment of the capital and the collapse of Liangzhu.'),
  ('Part of a wider crisis (interpretation)','The collapse is sometimes placed in or just before the c. 4.2-kiloyear climate event credited with stressing other early civilisations (Indus, Old Kingdom Egypt, Akkad), though the timing and causes differ by region.'),
  ('Pattern note (timeline)','Chinese tradition places the great flood tamed by Gun and Yu in roughly the same late-third-millennium era (see Gun-Yu flood); no source cited here connects Liangzhu to that story directly.')],
 ['doi:10.1126/sciadv.abi9275','url:https://whc.unesco.org/en/list/1592|Archaeological Ruins of Liangzhu City (UNESCO)|institution','wiki:Liangzhu_culture'],'Liangzhu_culture',end=-2300,
 changed='1936: first excavated by Shi Xingeng. 2007: city wall base announced. 2019: UNESCO listing. 2021: flood/rainfall collapse study.'),
X('tarim-mummies','Tarim mummies: "Europeans" in the desert?',H,'confirmed',2,-2135,['archaeological','genetic','radiocarbon'],
 'Hundreds of naturally mummified bodies from cemeteries around the Taklamakan Desert in the Tarim Basin, Xinjiang, China, many with intact hair (blond, red or brown), woollen textiles and tattoos. The oldest known burials are at Gumugou (c. 2135–1939 BCE), Xiaohe (c. 1884–1736 BCE) and Beifang (c. 1785–1664 BCE); Xiaohe, found by local hunter Ördek in 1911 and excavated by Folke Bergman in 1934, was lost for over 60 years before being relocated by GPS in 2000. Later, Iron Age mummies such as those of the Subeshi culture resemble the Saka (Scythian) Pazyryk culture.',
 [('Western migrants (earlier view)','Their features and twill and tartan-like textiles (compared by Elizabeth Wayland Barber with Hallstatt finds in Austria) led Mallory and Mair (2000) and others to see "Proto-Europoid" migrants, possibly early speakers of Tocharian Indo-European languages.'),
  ('Local isolated population (2021 genomes)','Zhang et al. (2021) sequenced the oldest mummies and found mostly Ancient North Eurasian ancestry with some Ancient Northeast Asian ancestry and no detectable Western Steppe ancestry: a genetically isolated local population that adopted neighbouring herding and farming practices. Later Tarim groups do show steppe and other ancestries, reflecting several waves of movement.')],
 ['doi:10.1038/s41586-021-04052-7','wiki:Tarim_mummies'],'Xiaohe_Cemetery',
 dateNote='Earliest c. 2135–1939 BCE; burials continue into the 1st millennium BCE',
 changed='1990s–2000s: widely presented as Western/Indo-European migrants. 2003: craniometrics fail to show close European affinity. 2021: ancient genomes point to an isolated local population.'),
X('roopkund','Roopkund: the Skeleton Lake',H,'confirmed',2,800,['archaeological','genetic','radiocarbon','oral tradition'],
 'A small glacial lake at 5,020 m in the Himalayas of Uttarakhand, India, where the remains of more than 300 people lie at the shore and on the bottom. Rediscovered by forest ranger Hari Kishan Madhwal in 1942 (British authorities first feared a hidden Japanese force), the bones come with wooden artefacts, iron spearheads, leather slippers and rings. A 2019 study combining radiocarbon dates and genomes of 38 individuals found three groups: 23 with South Asian ancestry who died around 800 CE (apparently over an extended period), and, around 1800 CE, 14 people with eastern Mediterranean ancestry (like people of mainland Greece and Crete) and one with Southeast Asian ancestry, who died in a single event.',
 [('Hailstorm legend','Local songs tell that Raja Jasdhaval of Kanauj, his pregnant wife Rani Balampa and their retinue, on pilgrimage to the Nanda Devi shrine, were killed by giant hailstones. 1950s studies of skulls reported injuries from round objects from above, which some took to confirm the legend; an Oxford radiocarbon date gave 850 CE ± 30.'),
  ('Still unexplained','The genetic results rule out a single catastrophe for all the dead, and it remains unknown why people of Mediterranean ancestry died at this remote lake around 1800. The Nanda Devi Raj Jat festival, held once every twelve years, takes place at Roopkund.')],
 ['doi:10.1038/s41467-019-11357-9','wiki:Roopkund'],'Roopkund',end=1800,
 changed='1942: skeletons noticed. 1950s: hailstorm interpretation. 2000s: single-date ~850 CE. 2019: genomes show three groups dying about 1,000 years apart.'),
X('hou-yi','Hou Yi shoots down the ten suns',M,'myth-legend',2,-2300,['mythological','textual'],
 'In Chinese mythology there were once ten suns (in some versions sons of the Jade Emperor) that crossed the sky one at a time until they all rose together and scorched the earth. The archer Hou Yi, sent by the sage-emperor Yao, shot them down one by one; each fell as a three-legged crow, until only one sun was spared. Hou Yi also killed monsters plaguing humanity. His wife Chang\'e swallowed the elixir of immortality and flew to the Moon, a story remembered at the Mid-Autumn Festival. The Chu Ci "Heavenly Questions" already refers to Yi being sent to reform the people of Xia.',
 [('Historical ruler','Classical sources such as the Bamboo Annals also describe a Yi who seized the Xia capital from King Taikang and was later deposed by his lieutenant Han Zhuo; the archer and the usurper are usually conflated.'),
  ('Textual origin','Recent research suggests the "shooting ten suns" story may have arisen from a miswriting of Yi shooting Xiang, a Xia king.'),
  ('Pattern note (timeline)','Successive or multiple suns also appear in the Aztec Five Suns (see entry), where each world age ends with its sun.')],
 ['wiki:Hou_Yi'],'Hou_Yi',
 dateNote='Set in the legendary reign of Emperor Yao (traditional 2357–2256 BCE) and early Xia'),
X('dangun','Dangun, grandson of heaven, and his "found" tomb',M,'myth-legend',2,-2333,['mythological','textual','archaeological'],
 'Korea\'s founding myth, first recorded in the 13th-century Samguk yusa: Hwanung, son of the Lord of Heaven Hwanin, descended to a sacred mountain with 3,000 followers and ministers of cloud, rain and wind, and taught laws, medicine and agriculture. A bear that endured 100 days in a cave on mugwort and garlic became a woman, and their son Dangun founded Gojoseon, the first Korean kingdom, traditionally in 2333 BCE. National Foundation Day (3 October) celebrates it in South Korea, which counted years in the Dangi era until 1961.',
 [('Real tomb (North Korean claim)','After Kim Il Sung insisted Dangun was a historical person, North Korean archaeologists announced in October 1993 that they had found his tomb near Pyongyang, with bones of a man and a woman dated by electron spin resonance to over 5,011 years ago; a stone step pyramid was built over it in 1994. North Korea now treats the myth as history.'),
  ('Sceptical view','Outside observers consider the data compromised by politics and nationalism; no independent testing has been allowed. A nail of the Goguryeo period (37 BCE–668 CE) was found, and South Korean historians think it more likely the tomb of a Goguryeo-era regional leader.'),
  ('Symbolic reading','Scholars often read the bear and tiger as two tribes seeking the favour of a heavenly prince, and the myth as an ideology of Gojoseon kingship.')],
 ['wiki:Dangun','wiki:Mausoleum_of_Tangun'],'Mausoleum_of_Tangun',
 dateNote='Traditional founding date 2333 BCE (Tongguk t\'onggam, 1485); North Korea says early 30th century BCE',
 changed='13th century: earliest written version (Samguk yusa). 1993: North Korea announces Dangun\'s tomb. 1994: mausoleum pyramid built.'),
X('cheomseongdae','Cheomseongdae star-gazing tower',H,'confirmed',2,632,['architectural','textual'],
 'A 9.17 m stone tower in Gyeongju, South Korea, built in the Silla kingdom under Queen Seondeok (r. 632–647). Its name means "star-gazing tower", and it is often described as the oldest surviving astronomical observatory in Asia, possibly the world. A square base of 12 stones supports a curved cylindrical body with a square window halfway up and a square top; an observation device is presumed to have stood on top. It is part of the Gyeongju Historic Areas UNESCO World Heritage Site and now tilts slightly to the north-east.',
 [('Calendar symbolism (interpretation)','The body is said to use 365 cut stones, one per day of the year, and the window divides it into 12 courses above and below, read as months and the 24 solar terms; the 12 base stones may also stand for months.'),
  ('Counting dispute','Documents report different numbers of stones; a 1962 survey by Hong Sa-jun counted 366, so the "365" symbolism depends on how stones are counted.')],
 ['url:https://whc.unesco.org/en/list/976|Gyeongju Historic Areas (UNESCO)|institution','wiki:Cheomseongdae'],'Cheomseongdae',end=647,
 dateNote='Built during the reign of Queen Seondeok (632–647)'),
X('xin-zhui','Lady Dai (Xin Zhui): the soft-skinned mummy',H,'confirmed',2,-168,['archaeological','medical'],
 'Xin Zhui, Marchioness of Dai, wife of the chancellor of the Changsha Kingdom in the Western Han dynasty, died about 169 or 168 BCE aged about 50. Her tomb at Mawangdui, Changsha, found by workers digging an air-raid shelter near a hospital and excavated from January 1972 with the help of over 1,500 local high-school students, held four nested coffins, about 1,400 artefacts and more than 150 kinds of food. Her body is one of the best-preserved ever found: skin still soft and moist, joints flexible, organs and blood vessels intact with traces of type A blood. An autopsy found arteriosclerosis, gallstones, schistosomiasis and 130 melon seeds in her digestive tract, showing she died in summer within hours of eating melon.',
 [('How was she preserved?','Scientists point to careful preparation, airtight nested coffins and a deep chamber sealed with charcoal and white clay, which kept water and air out and the temperature cool; an acidic liquid containing salt and magnesium surrounded the body, possibly from the body itself. They do not know for certain which combination of factors worked.'),
  ('Not unique','Other "Mawangdui-type" bodies have been found in China (Sui Xiaoyuan in Jingzhou, Ling Huiping in Lianyungang), also in liquid-filled coffins, but no consistent explanation fits all of them.')],
 ['wiki:Xin_Zhui','wiki:Mawangdui'],'Xin_Zhui',
 dateNote='Died 169 or 168 BCE; tomb found 1968–71, excavated from 1972'),
X('imperial-regalia','The hidden Imperial Regalia of Japan',H,'disputed',2,690,['textual','mythological'],
 'The Three Sacred Treasures of Japan: the sword Kusanagi no Tsurugi, the mirror Yata no Kagami and the jewel Yasakani no Magatama. Since 690 their presentation to the emperor has been central to the enthronement, but the ceremony is not public and by tradition the objects are seen only by the emperor and certain priests. They are commonly thought to be kept at Atsuta Shrine (sword), the Imperial Palace sanctuaries (jewel) and Ise Grand Shrine (mirror). Their actual historical status is unknown because they are deliberately kept from view; even at the enthronements of 1990 and 2019 they stayed shrouded in packages or boxes. In July 1945 Emperor Hirohito ordered them protected "at all costs" and said he was ready to share their fate.',
 [('Divine origin (legend)','The sun goddess Amaterasu gave them to her grandson Ninigi when sending him to pacify Japan, and they passed to the first emperor, Jimmu. The mirror and jewel lured Amaterasu out of the cave where she had hidden and plunged the world into darkness; the sword was taken from the tail of the eight-headed serpent Yamata no Orochi.'),
  ('Lost and replaced?','The six-year-old Emperor Antoku and the regalia went into the sea at the Battle of Dan-no-ura (1185). Medieval texts disagree whether the sword was lost, was already a replica, or returned by supernatural means; replicas of the sword and mirror are known from as early as the 9th century.')],
 ['wiki:Imperial_Regalia_of_Japan'],'Imperial_Regalia_of_Japan',
 dateNote='Used in enthronements since 690; legendary origin in the age of the gods'),
X('laguna-copperplate','Laguna Copperplate Inscription',H,'confirmed',2,900,['textual','archaeological'],
 'The oldest known document from the Philippines: a copper plate about 20 × 30 cm, found in 1987 by a labourer dredging sand near the mouth of the Lumbang River in Lumban, Laguna. It is dated to the Shaka year 822, which corresponds to Monday 21 April 900 CE, and certifies that the children of one Namwaran were released from a debt of gold (1 kati and 8 suwarnas, about 865 g). Written mostly in Old Malay in the Kawi script, with Sanskrit technical words and Old Javanese or Old Tagalog honorifics, it names the polity of Tondo (Tundun) and other places. Sold to an antique dealer and then to the National Museum, it was translated by Dutch anthropologist Antoon Postma in 1991.',
 [('Javanese link?','Some historians link the toponym Medang (Mdang) to the Medang palace in Java; others note it is a common Malayo-Polynesian term. Postma himself left open that Mdang and Tondo might originally be Javanese place names.'),
  ('Local places','The Pila Historical Society Foundation and historian Jaime Tiongson argue that Pailah and Puliran refer to places near where the plate was found, citing a large precolonial settlement at Pila.'),
  ('Precolonial knowledge','It is the earliest evidence of mathematics in precolonial Philippine societies: a standard system of weights for gold and a date fixed by the phases of the Moon.')],
 ['wiki:Laguna_Copperplate_Inscription'],'Laguna_Copperplate_Inscription',
 changed='1987: found while dredging. 1991: translated by Antoon Postma; it is now the oldest known document from the Philippines and a key source on 10th-century polities such as Tondo.'),
# --- part 3: Philippines, Arabia, Sri Lanka, Japan, Pacific
X('tasaday','The Tasaday: Stone Age tribe or hoax?',H,'disputed',2,1971,['ethnographic','linguistic','journalistic'],
 'In 1971 Manuel Elizalde, head of the Philippine minorities agency PANAMIN and a crony of President Ferdinand Marcos, announced the discovery of the Tasaday, a small group in the rainforest of Mindanao said to live in caves with "Stone Age" tools in complete isolation. National Geographic covered them (cover story, August 1972), and visitors included Charles Lindbergh and Gina Lollobrigida. In 1972 Marcos declared a 77 km² reserve around their caves, and in 1976, under martial law, closed it to all visitors; no anthropologist had studied them for more than six weeks. Elizalde fled the country in 1983 amid allegations that he squandered foundation money.',
 [('Hoax (1986 claim)','After Marcos fell in 1986, Swiss journalist Oswald Iten and Joey Lozano made an unauthorised two-hour visit, found the caves deserted and claimed the Tasaday were members of known local tribes who had posed as cave-dwellers under pressure from Elizalde; some local people admitted pretending to be Tasaday for money and goods.'),
  ('Genuine but recent isolation','Linguist Lawrence Reid, after ten months of fieldwork (1993–1996), concluded they "probably were as isolated as they claim", unfamiliar with agriculture, with a distinct dialect, and that the original reporters perpetrated no hoax; he estimated their isolation lasted perhaps no more than 150 years. A widely held summary is that they were a separate forest-gathering group rarely in contact with neighbours, but not a Stone Age survival.')],
 ['wiki:Tasaday'],'Tasaday',
 changed='1971–72: hailed as a "Stone Age" tribe. 1986: denounced as a hoax. 1990s: linguistic work supports a real, recently isolated group.'),
X('iram-ubar','Iram of the Pillars and Ubar, "Atlantis of the Sands"',M,'disputed',2,610,['textual','archaeological'],
 'The Quran (Surah 89) mentions "Iram, who had lofty pillars" alongside the destroyed people of ʿĀd; later the Thousand and One Nights tells of the many-columned city of Iram. Ubar, a legendary land of southern Arabia, is often equated with it and nicknamed the Atlantis of the Sands. Interpretations range from a city (Alexandria, Damascus, or a city that moved) to a region (the biblical Aram) or a tribe whose "pillars" were tent poles. Inscriptions at the Temple of al-Lat at Wadi Ramm in Jordan mention Iram and the tribe of ʿĀd, making it a more plausible candidate for some scholars.',
 [('Found at Shisr? (Clapp)','Amateur archaeologist Nicholas Clapp proposed that Iram is Ubar and identified it with the archaeological site of Shisr in Dhofar, Oman, a former oasis trading post on the frankincense routes (part of the UNESCO Land of Frankincense since 2000). Scholars generally do not accept this, and Clapp himself later denied the Shisr identification.'),
  ('Source of the legend','Ubar is described by ancient and medieval authors as a land rather than a city; it has been suggested that the drying of the spring and abandonment of Shisr could have inspired the legend of a lost city.')],
 ['wiki:Iram_of_the_Pillars','wiki:Archaeological_site_of_Shisr','url:https://whc.unesco.org/en/list/1010|Land of Frankincense (UNESCO)|institution'],'Archaeological_site_of_Shisr',
 dateNote='Named in the Quran (7th century CE); the city or people it refers to are undated'),
X('tooth-relic','The tooth of the Buddha in Kandy',H,'disputed',2,310,['textual','religious tradition'],
 'Sri Lanka venerates a tooth of the Buddha at the Temple of the Sacred Tooth Relic (Sri Dalada Maligawa) in the former royal palace complex of Kandy, part of a UNESCO World Heritage Site. Tradition holds that after the Buddha\'s cremation his left canine was kept in Kalinga, India, and that when Kalinga was attacked Princess Hemamali and Prince Dantha carried it to Sri Lanka, hidden in her hair, in the reign of Sirimeghavanna of Anuradhapura (301–328). Because whoever held the relic was believed to hold the right to rule, kings kept it beside their palaces, moving it with each capital; it was hidden in a grinding stone at one point and carried away from Kandy during the Portuguese invasion of 1603. Today it lies within seven golden caskets and is honoured by the annual Esala Perahera procession.',
 [('Destroyed in Goa? (Portuguese account)','Portuguese chroniclers João de Barros and Diogo do Couto wrote that a tooth relic seized in the 1560 expedition against the Jaffna Kingdom was taken to Goa and destroyed, by burning and casting into the sea, on the viceroy Constantino de Bragança\'s orders.'),
  ('Survival (Sri Lankan tradition)','The relic venerated in Kandy is held by Buddhists to be the authentic tooth; the relationship between it and the relic destroyed in Goa is a matter of differing accounts. A 2024 survey found 32 museums and temples worldwide claiming one or more Buddha tooth relics.')],
 ['wiki:Relic_of_the_tooth_of_the_Buddha','wiki:Temple_of_the_Tooth','url:https://whc.unesco.org/en/list/450|Sacred City of Kandy (UNESCO)|institution'],'Temple_of_the_Tooth',
 dateNote='Tradition: arrived in Sri Lanka in the reign of Sirimeghavanna (301–328 CE)'),
X('ningyo-mermaids','Mermaid mummies of Japan and the "Fiji mermaid"',H,'debunked',2,1736,['physical analysis','textual','radiocarbon'],
 'Japanese tradition knows the ningyo ("human-fish"); the oldest pictures (scroll copy of 1069) show one presented to Prince Shōtoku in the 7th century, and some temples keep preserved "mermaids" tied to such legends. In the Edo period craftsmen made them commercially: a Dutch official on Dejima (1820s) saw "mermaids", two-headed men and dragons for sale. One reportedly bought from Japanese sailors in 1822 became the "Fiji mermaid" (a monkey\'s torso sewn to a fish), shown in London that year and by P. T. Barnum in New York in 1842. In 2022–23 researchers from Kurashiki University of Science and the Arts scanned the "mermaid mummy" of Enjuin temple, Asakuchi (Okayama), which came with a note saying it was netted off Tosa (Kōchi) between 1736 and 1740.',
 [('Result of the 2023 study','X-ray CT, microscopy and radiocarbon dating showed no skeleton except fish jaws and fin bones: the body is cloth, paper and cotton with plaster-like material, covered with layered paper, pufferfish skin and animal hair above and croaker-fish skin below; scales date it to the late 19th century, and no DNA was found.'),
  ('Real animals behind the legend?','Proposed sources for ningyo lore include giant salamanders (the earliest were caught in fresh water), dugongs or Steller\'s sea cows, seals, and oarfish (whose red crest matches some descriptions).')],
 ['wiki:Ningyo','wiki:Fiji_mermaid','url:https://www.kusa.ac.jp/news/2023/02/20230207mermaid.html|Kurashiki University of Science and the Arts: final report on the Enjuin mermaid mummy (2023, Japanese)|institution','url:https://www.asahi.com/ajw/articles/14834950|Asahi Shimbun: Study finds "mermaid mummy" largely a molded object (2023)|news'],'Fiji_mermaid',end=2023,
 changed='1822: Fiji mermaid exhibited in London. 1842: Barnum shows it in New York. 2023: CT study shows a Japanese temple mermaid is a made object of the late 1800s.'),
X('rongorongo','Rongorongo: the undeciphered script of Rapa Nui',H,'disputed',2,1450,['textual','radiocarbon'],
 'A system of glyphs on about two dozen wooden objects from Easter Island (Rapa Nui), collected in the late 19th century and now scattered among museums; none remain on the island. The glyphs (outlines of humans, animals, plants and shapes) run in reverse boustrophedon, every other line upside down. Apart from part of one tablet that relates to a lunar calendar and a possible genealogy, none can be read. Oral history says only an elite was literate and the tablets were sacred; the scribes and their knowledge were lost when Rapa Nui society collapsed after Peruvian slave raids and epidemics in the 1860s. A 2024 radiocarbon study of tablets in Rome securely dated the wood of one to the mid-15th century, though the carving could be younger.',
 [('Independent invention','If rongorongo is true writing and was invented on the island before European contact, it would be one of very few independent inventions of writing in human history; the 2024 date supports pre-contact use.'),
  ('Inspired by Europeans','Others suggest the islanders created it after seeing writing during the Spanish treaty-signing visit of 1770; some tablets are carved on European oars or South African yellowwood, and the script was not reported by early explorers (possibly because it was taboo).'),
  ('Fanciful readings','As with most undeciphered scripts, there are many fanciful interpretations and claimed translations; scholars accept none of them.')],
 ['doi:10.1038/s41598-024-53063-7','wiki:Rongorongo','url:https://whc.unesco.org/en/list/715|Rapa Nui National Park (UNESCO)|institution'],'Rongorongo',
 dateNote='Wood of one tablet dated to the mid-15th century (2024); texts collected from the 1860s',
 changed='1860s: tablets first reported by missionaries. 1958: Barthel publishes the corpus. 2024: radiocarbon dating suggests pre-contact origin for at least one tablet.'),
X('roi-mata','Roi Mata: a legend that led to a grave',H,'confirmed',2,1600,['oral tradition','archaeological'],
 'Roi Mata was a chiefly title in central Vanuatu borne by a dynasty from the 11th to the 17th century; the most famous holder, often simply called Roi Mata, lived around 1600. Oral tradition says he united the tribes, ruled peacefully, was poisoned by his brother, and was buried away from his homeland on the island of Eretoka, where his name was then avoided for fear of his spirit. In 1967 French archaeologist José Garanger used these stories to locate the grave on Eretoka: an elaborate burial with Roi Mata and over 25 members of his retinue. Three associated sites on Efate, Lelepa and Eretoka (his residence, the site of his death and the burial site) became UNESCO\'s Chief Roi Mata\'s Domain in 2008.',
 [('Oral history as evidence','The find shows oral tradition guiding archaeologists to a burial of about 370 years earlier, including details such as a mass burial of the chief\'s retinue.')],
 ['url:https://whc.unesco.org/en/list/1280|Chief Roi Mata\'s Domain (UNESCO)|institution',"wiki:Chief_Roi_Mata's_Domain"],"Chief_Roi_Mata's_Domain",
 dateNote='The last Roi Mata lived around 1600; grave located in 1967',
 changed='1967: Garanger finds the grave using oral tradition. 2008: UNESCO World Heritage listing.'),
X('haamonga','Haʻamonga ʻa Maui, the "Stonehenge of the Pacific"',H,'disputed',2,1200,['archaeological','oral tradition','archaeoastronomy'],
 'A coral-limestone trilithon at Niutōua on Tongatapu, Tonga: two uprights about 5.2 m high, each weighing about 30–40 tonnes above ground, with a lintel set into deep mortises. Historical analysis places it around 1200 CE under Tuʻitātui, the eleventh Tuʻi Tonga, probably as a gateway to the royal compound, representing his two sons joined by the lintel. Nearby is a stone "throne" the king leaned against to avoid being attacked from behind. A national park since 1972.',
 [('Built by Māui (tradition)','Traditional accounts say the hero Māui made it, carrying the stones from ʻUvea in a giant canoe, as they were too big for mortals; its name means "the burden of Māui". Some believe it points to Pulotu, the land of the gods.'),
  ('Solar calendar?','In 1967 King Taufaʻahau Tupou IV concluded it marked the sunrise at the solstices and equinoxes, a view supported by historian Tevita Fale, who described a V-shaped mark on the lintel aligned with these sunrises.'),
  ('Sceptical view','Astronomer Kik Velt of ʻAtenisi Institute argued the V-mark is only about 10 cm long and points along the lintel\'s main axis, too short to be a reliable direction marker.')],
 ['wiki:Haʻamonga_ʻa_Maui'],'Haʻamonga_ʻa_Maui',
 dateNote='Around 1200 CE (traditional history)'),
X('seven-sisters','The Seven Sisters and the lost Pleiad',M,'hypothesis',2,-100000,['mythological','astronomical','speculative'],
 'Many cultures call the Pleiades star cluster the "seven sisters" although most people see six stars, and many tell a story to explain the missing seventh. In Greek myth the Pleiades are daughters of Atlas, turned into stars to escape the hunter Orion; one sister went into hiding. Across Aboriginal Australia the cluster is a group of young women pursued by a man or men in Orion, and one sister has died, hidden or been abducted. Folklorist Stith Thompson noted the cluster is "nearly always imagined" as seven sisters. The cluster appears on the Nebra sky disc and heads Babylonian star lists, and Japan\'s 8th-century Kojiki calls it Mutsuraboshi, "six stars".',
 [('100,000-year-old story (Norris & Norris)','Astronomers Ray and Barnaby Norris (2021) used Gaia star motions to show that about 100,000 years ago the star Pleione was farther from Atlas and easy to see, so the cluster would have shown seven stars; they suggest the stories could date back to before modern humans left Africa, which would make them perhaps the oldest stories on Earth.'),
  ('European diffusion (older view)','Anthropologists once thought Europeans brought the Greek story to Australia, where it was adapted; Norris notes the Aboriginal stories appear much older than European contact.'),
  ('Independent origins (interpretation)','Because the cluster is a conspicuous group of six or so stars, similar stories could also have arisen independently in different cultures; the deep-time explanation remains a hypothesis.')],
 ['doi:10.1007/978-3-030-64606-6_11','doi:10.64628/aa.thef4qfdd','wiki:Pleiades'],'Pleiades',
 dateNote='Proposed origin about 100,000 years ago (speculative); recorded versions are much later'),
X('menehune','Menehune: Hawaiʻi\'s hidden builders',M,'myth-legend',2,1820,['oral tradition','textual'],
 'In Hawaiian tradition the Menehune are a race of small, dwarf-like people who live hidden in deep forests and valleys and are superb craftspeople, said to have lived in Hawaiʻi before settlers came from elsewhere in Polynesia. They work only at night and abandon any project not finished by dawn; only their children and people connected to them can see them. Structures attributed to them include the Alekoko (Menehune) fishpond wall at Niumalu and the Kīkīaola ditch at Waimea on Kauaʻi, structures on Necker Island and Ulupō Heiau on Oʻahu.',
 [('Remembered first settlers','Some early scholars proposed a first settlement from the Marquesas and a second from Tahiti, whose newcomers oppressed the "commoners" (manahune in Tahitian), who fled to the mountains; supporters cite a census of Kauaʻi ordered by Kaumualiʻi around 1820 that listed 65 people as menehune.'),
  ('Post-contact legend','Folklorist Katharine Luomala argued the small-people legends are a post-European adaptation of the term manahune ("lowly people") to European tales of brownies; others reply that absence from early records proves little for an oral tradition.')],
 ['wiki:Menehune'],'Alekoko_Fishpond',
 dateNote='Traditions recorded from the 19th century; 1820 census claim'),
X('kaimanawa-wall','The Kaimanawa "wall"',H,'debunked',2,1996,['geological','archaeological'],
 'A line of block-like stones in the Kaimanawa State Forest south of Lake Taupō, New Zealand. In May 1996 the NZ Listener and the Holmes TV show featured archaeologist and historian Barry Brailsford\'s view that it was "the best evidence so far" of a pre-Māori "Waitaha nation" settled 2,000 years ago, because nothing like it exists in Māori culture. Department of Conservation archaeologist Neville Ritchie examined it on 7 May 1996 and found matching micro-irregularities across the joints and imperfect joint alignments, showing the "blocks" were one rock. Geologist Peter Wood identified it as jointed Rangitaiki ignimbrite, a 330,000-year-old volcanic rock that cracks this way as it cools.',
 [('Pre-Māori civilisation (claim)','The idea that the wall was built by an earlier culture remains popular in alternative literature; Tūwharetoa people refer to the place in oral tradition as a kōhatu (rock).'),
  ('Natural formation (finding)','Both specialists who inspected it found no evidence of human working; Te Ara, the encyclopedia of New Zealand, describes it as a natural phenomenon.')],
 ['url:https://teara.govt.nz/en/document/1603/the-kaimanawa-wall|Te Ara: The Kaimanawa wall|encyclopedia','url:https://articles.skeptics.nz/1996/11/01/a-new-age-myth-the-kaimanawa-wall/|Ritchie, N. A. (1996). A New Age Myth: the Kaimanawa Wall. NZ Skeptics|article','wiki:Kaimanawa_Range'],None,
 changed='May 1996: claimed as a megalith; within weeks archaeological and geological inspections conclude it is natural ignimbrite.'),
]
